// WHY THIS EXISTS
// Artwork used to be posted to api/promopro/art.js as base64 inside a JSON
// body. A Vercel function refuses any request body over 4.5 MB, and base64
// inflates a file by a third, so the real ceiling was about 3.3 MB against a
// QuickBooks attachment limit of 20 MB. Uploading straight from the browser
// to blob storage skips the function and its limit entirely.
//
// Hand-rolling that upload was the alternative and was rejected: it means
// depending on internal request headers (x-api-version, x-vercel-blob-store-id)
// that Vercel can change in any release, with no deprecation to notice.
//
// WHY BUNDLED RATHER THAN COPIED FROM node_modules
// The published files import five npm packages plus three Node builtins by
// bare name, which a browser with no build step cannot resolve. Bundling
// once, here, produces one file with no imports at all and keeps the repo's
// no-build-step rule intact.
//
// HOW TO REGENERATE, when the SDK is upgraded:
//   echo 'export { upload, uploadPresigned } from "@vercel/blob/client";' > entry.js
//   npx esbuild entry.js --bundle --format=esm --platform=browser \
//       --minify --define:process.env={} --outfile=vendor/blob-client.js
//
// Verified before committing: loads with no process, Buffer or require in
// scope, has no external imports, and exports exactly `upload` and
// `uploadPresigned`. Both are needed: which one applies depends on how the
// Blob store is connected. See api/promopro/art-upload.js.
// test/blob-client-vendor.test.cjs re-checks all of that on every run.

